  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe strategy employs a targeted gradient ascent approach to improve the step function h by focusing on the shift k that currently causes the maximum overlap. By iteratively adjusting h to reduce this maximum overlap, the method aims to minimize C\u2085 more effectively than global search methods. This approach alternates between identifying the worst-case shift and refining h to mitigate that specific overlap, ensuring a focused optimization effort.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\n- Variables: h \u2208 [0, 1]^N, where N = n_points, sum(h) = N / 2\n- Objective: Minimize C\u2085 = max_k [\u222bh(x)(1 - h(x + k)) dx]\n- Constraints: 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 and \u222bh(x) dx = 1 over [0,2]\n- Implementation as a targeted gradient ascent problem, focusing on the worst shift k at each iteration.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Initialization:\n   1. Start with initial_h_values if available; otherwise, initialize h uniformly with sum(h) = N / 2.\nb. Shift grid setup:\n   1. Discretize shifts k \u2208 [-2, 2] into a grid (e.g., 100 shifts).\nc. Main optimization loop:\n   1. For current h, compute correlations C(k) for all k in grid.\n   2. Identify worst_k (shift with maximum C\u2085).\n   3. Compute gradient of C\u2085 at worst_k.\n   4. Update h using gradient step: h += learning_rate * gradient.\n   5. Repair h to satisfy constraints.\n   6. Optionally perturb h slightly to escape local optima.\n   7. Refine shift grid around worst_k for better approximation.\n   8. Repeat until budget exhausted or convergence.\nd. Gradient computation:\n   1. For each h[j], gradient = -2 / N * (h[j + k] + h[j - k]) if indices valid.\ne. Repair mechanism:\n   1. Clip h to [0, 1].\n   2. Adjust elements to meet sum(h) = N / 2 by proportionally distributing delta.\nf. Termination:\n   1. Stop when time budget nears exhaustion or no improvement in C\u2085 for iterations.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\n- Initial grid size: 100 shifts, adaptive refinement.\n- Learning rate: Adaptive, halved every 100 iterations if no improvement.\n- Perturbation: Small random noise added every 50 iterations.\n- Repair method: Proportional distribution of delta after clipping.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Each iteration involves N^2 operations for correlation, manageable for N=100.\n- Gradient steps and repairs are O(N), feasible within 600s for thousands of iterations.\n- Backup solutions stored after each major refinement.\n- Robust to local minima due to focused gradient steps and periodic perturbations.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Ensure h[i] \u2208 [0,1] and sum(h) = N / 2.\n- Compute C\u2085 using evaluator; return best_h, c5_bound, and n_points.\n- Sanity checks for finite C\u2085 and feasibility.",
